  "prediction": "The Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) has a complex system for addressing employee misconduct, involving multiple offices and information systems. The report highlights several issues with the VA's system for collecting and analyzing data on employee misconduct, including:\n\n1. 12 fragmented information systems that collect employee misconduct and disciplinary actions, but none of which contain complete information.\n2. Lack of standardization and identifiers in data files, making it difficult to analyze and compare data across systems.\n3. Inconsistent retention of records, with some offices misinterpreting OPM and NARA guidance on record retention periods.\n4. Inadequate documentation of disciplinary actions, with 10% of files missing and 22 out of 36 file requirements not consistently demonstrated.\n5. Inconsistent adherence to VA's policy for documenting procedures followed in the adjudication of misconduct cases.\n\nThe report also found that VA facility and program offices did not consistently follow policies and procedures established by the Office of Inspector General (OIG) for investigating allegations of misconduct involving senior officials. Specifically:\n\n1. 4 out of 23 cases did not contain evidence of an independent review by an official separate from and at a higher pay grade than the accused.\n2. 7 cases did not provide a clear indication of whether all allegations were substantiated or unsubstantiated.\n3. 17 cases did not provide supporting documentation used in their review.\n\nThe report also analyzed data from the OAR Legacy Referral Tracking List and VA Office of Inspector General (OIG) case-referral and investigative case-management systems, and found that:\n\n1. 11 fields out of 92 in the OAR Legacy Referral Tracking List were missing information that could be used to analyze misconduct.\n2. 9 fields out of 21 in the VA Office of Accountability and Whistleblower Protection (OAWP) database were missing information that could be used to analyze misconduct.\n\nThe report concludes that the VA's system for addressing employee misconduct is fragmented and lacks standardization, making it difficult to analyze and compare data across systems. The report recommends that the VA take steps to improve its system for collecting and analyzing data on employee misconduct, including:\n\n1. Developing a comprehensive and reliable information system to track employee misconduct and disciplinary actions.\n2. Standardizing data fields and identifiers across systems.\n3. Improving documentation of disciplinary actions and procedures followed in the adjudication of misconduct cases.\n4. Ensuring that VA facility and program offices follow policies and procedures established by the OIG for investigating allegations of misconduct involving senior officials.\n\nOverall, the report",
